---
/**
 * The mark on a link that leaves the site: a small arrow, plus the same fact
 * said out loud for anyone who cannot see it.
 *
 * The arrow is aria-hidden and the words are visually hidden, so each reader
 * gets the version they can use and neither gets it twice. Without the words, a
 * screen reader announces the link and then a new tab takes focus with no
 * warning, which is the disorienting case WCAG 3.2.5 is about.
 *
 * Drawn as an inline SVG rather than the ↗ character. As text it inherits the
 * font, so it lands at a different size and baseline in the serif body than in
 * the sans footer, and some fonts have no glyph for it at all.
 */
---

<svg
  class="ext"
  viewBox="0 0 10 10"
  width="10"
  height="10"
  aria-hidden="true"
  focusable="false"
>
  <path
    d="M3 1h6v6M9 1L1 9"
    fill="none"
    stroke="currentColor"
    stroke-width="1.4"
    stroke-linecap="round"
    stroke-linejoin="round"
  />
</svg><span class="ext-sr">(opens in a new tab)</span>

<style>
  .ext {
    /* Nudged up to sit against the cap height rather than the baseline, and
       given a hair of left margin so it never touches the final letter. */
    display: inline-block;
    margin-left: 0.18em;
    vertical-align: 0.08em;
    /* Scales with whatever it sits in: 10px next to 13px footer text, larger in
       an 18px paragraph. A fixed size looks wrong in one of the two. */
    width: 0.62em;
    height: 0.62em;
    /* Quieter than the link text. The arrow is a hint, not part of the label. */
    opacity: 0.55;
  }

  a:hover .ext,
  a:focus-visible .ext {
    opacity: 1;
  }

  /* Never let the arrow carry the underline: it reads as a broken glyph. */
  .ext {
    text-decoration: none;
  }

  .ext-sr {
    position: absolute;
    width: 1px;
    height: 1px;
    padding: 0;
    margin: -1px;
    overflow: hidden;
    clip-path: inset(50%);
    white-space: nowrap;
    border: 0;
  }
</style>
